          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Jane\",\"addressee\":\"St John\",\"anchor_quote\":\"There is no dishonour, no breach of promise, no desertion in the case.\\nI am not under the slightest obligation to go to India, especially with\\nstrangers. With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you; but I am convinced that, go\\nwhen and with whom I would, I should not live long in that climate.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you (as a sister).\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Jane directly avows a loving emotional state, expressly qualifying it as sisterly love. The surrounding statement presents that affection, admiration, and trust as her reasons for having been willing to venture much with St John.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":1,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.86,\"reason\":\"The expression weakly affirms a sisterly relational bond and is connected to Jane's willingness to venture much with St John. It does not, however, create or renew a clear present obligation: in this very turn Jane denies being bound to go to India and frames the venture conditionally and retrospectively. Her earlier offers to serve as his assistant or curate are related but distinct undertakings, not promises made by the focal words themselves.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I am not under the slightest obligation to go to India, especially with\\nstrangers.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I say again, I will be your\\ncurate, if you like, but never your wife.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The focal expression occurs within a controlled, reasoned reply distinguishing obligation, trust, sisterly affection, and practical danger. Nothing in the supplied scene presents these words as a reflex-like affective discharge rather than a deliberate avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"There is no dishonour, no breach of promise, no desertion in the case.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you; but I am convinced that, go\\nwhen and with whom I would, I should not live long in that climate.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing affection, the expression has a moderate contrastive and relationship-defining function. By saying \\\"as a sister,\\\" Jane identifies the kind of love she bears St John and reinforces the boundary between sisterly companionship and the marital role she has refused.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I say again, I will be your\\ncurate, if you like, but never your wife.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Jane is rebutting St John's claim that she would dishonourably break a promise or desert an engagement to go to India. She explains that she has no such obligation, contrasts St John with strangers, and avows sisterly love as part of explaining why she had been willing to venture much with him, while maintaining her refusal to become his wife and raising the danger of the climate.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The occurrence weakly invokes Jane's sisterly bond with St John and explains a conditional willingness to undertake hardship with him, but it does not itself clearly promise travel, marriage, or service. Jane expressly denies an obligation to go to India, and the surrounding dialogue distinguishes her separate offer to accompany or assist him from her refusal to marry him. St John does not acknowledge or accept the sisterly affirmation; he answers by accusing her of fear and continues to challenge her decision.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as Charlotte Brontë's Jane Eyre. The classification relies only on the supplied evidence.\",\"limitations\":\"The scene strongly supports the avowal's sisterly and boundary-setting force, but it does not conclusively establish the full terms of any ongoing sibling-like commitment or St John's uptake of that relational positioning. It also cannot independently prove Jane's sincerity beyond the textual evidence presented.\"}"
        }
